1
00:04:10,714 --> 00:04:16,714
You'll beWITCHED! You'll beDAZZLED! You'll be swept into a
world of enchantment BEYOND ANYTHING BEFORE!

2
00:08:21,428 --> 00:08:27,428
Bedknobs and Broomsticks is a adventure, fantasy,
comedy film directed by Robert Stevenson.

3
00:12:32,142 --> 00:12:38,142
Three children evacuated from London during World War II are
forced to stay with an eccentric spinster.

4
00:16:42,857 --> 00:16:48,857
The children's initial fears disappear when they find
out she is in fact a trainee witch.

5
00:20:53,571 --> 00:20:59,571
Miss Price, played by Angela Lansbury.

6
00:25:04,285 --> 00:25:10,285
Emelius, played by David Tomlinson.

7
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:21,000
Mr. Jelk, played by Roddy McDowall.

8
00:33:25,714 --> 00:33:31,714
Bookman, played by Sam Jaffe.

9
00:37:36,428 --> 00:37:42,428
Col. Heller, played by John Ericson.

10
00:41:47,142 --> 00:41:53,142
Swinburne, played by Bruce Forsyth.

11
00:45:57,857 --> 00:46:03,857
Bedknobs and Broomsticks is a 1971 American live-action/animated hybrid musical comedy fantasy film directed by Robert
Stevenson from a screenplay by Bill Walsh and Don DaGradi and with songs written by the Sherman Brothers.

12
00:50:08,571 --> 00:50:14,571
It was produced by Walsh for Walt Disney Productions.

13
00:54:19,285 --> 00:54:25,285
It is based upon the books The Magic Bedknob (1943) and Bonfires and
Broomsticks (1947) by English author Mary Norton.

14
00:58:30,000 --> 00:58:36,000
It combines live action and animation, and stars Angela Lansbury, David
Tomlinson, Ian Weighill, Cindy O'Callaghan, and Roy Snart.

15
01:02:40,714 --> 01:02:46,714
During the early 1960s, the film entered development when the negotiations for
the film rights to Mary Poppins (1964) were placed on hold.

16
01:06:51,428 --> 01:06:57,428
When the rights were acquired, the film was shelved repeatedly because of its
similarities to Mary Poppins until it was revived in 1969.

17
01:11:02,142 --> 01:11:08,142
Originally at a length of 139 minutes, it was edited down to almost
two hours before its premiere at Radio City Music Hall.

18
01:15:12,857 --> 01:15:18,857
The film was released on December 13, 1971, to mixed reviews from film
critics, some of whom praised the live-action/animated sequence.

19
01:19:23,571 --> 01:19:29,571
It received five Academy Awards nominations, winning
one for Best Special Visual Effects.

20
01:23:34,285 --> 01:23:40,285
It was the last film released before the death of
Walt Disney's surviving brother, Roy O.

21
01:27:45,000 --> 01:27:51,000
Disney, who died one week later.

22
01:31:55,714 --> 01:32:01,714
It was also the last theatrical film Reginald Owen appeared in before his death the following
year on November 5, 1972; his last two acting credits were for television.

23
01:36:06,428 --> 01:36:12,428
It was also the last film work of screenwriter Don DaGradi before
his retirement in 1970 and death on August 4, 1991.

24
01:40:17,142 --> 01:40:23,142
In 1996, the film was restored with most of the
deleted material re-inserted back into it.

25
01:44:27,857 --> 01:44:33,857
A stage musical adaptation of it had its world premiere at the Theatre Royal in Newcastle upon
Tyne on 14 August 2021 before embarking on a UK and Ireland tour until May 2022.

26
01:48:38,571 --> 01:48:44,571
The film explores themes of witch, flying, nazi, world war ii, knight.

27
01:52:49,285 --> 01:52:55,285
The tone is lighthearted.
